  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 4xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}+2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2{x}^{2}+2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 2yz\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+1 & ={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+z+C.\n$$",
